    "question": {
      ":question/id": "424dd11a-34ef-5871-902d-30b4ade4138e",
      ":question/problem": "Which diagram represents\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(y-2x\\right)\\,i+\\left(2y-4x\\right)\\,j\n$$\n\nat $A=(-1,1)$, $B=(-2,-2)$, $C=(3,3)$? All diagrams use one unit per grid interval.\n\nEvaluate both components at each point, then place the tail of its vector at that point.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$. Substitute into each component, then combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-1,1)&=(1)-(2)(-1)=3 \\\\\nQ(-1,1)&=(2)(1)-(2)(2)(-1)=6 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(-1,1)&=3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\\nP(-2,-2)&=(-2)-(2)(-2)=2 \\\\\nQ(-2,-2)&=(2)(-2)-(2)(2)(-2)=4 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(-2,-2)&=2\\,i+4\\,j \\\\\nP(3,3)&=(3)-(2)(3)=-3 \\\\\nQ(3,3)&=(2)(3)-(2)(2)(3)=-6 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(3,3)&=-3\\,i-6\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first component gives the horizontal displacement and the second gives the vertical displacement. Place each tail at its labeled point. A zero component gives no displacement along that axis; a zero vector stays at its tail.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/edb-math/repairs/2026-10-28-topic-audit-3/published-probe/17592186172083/543aa249dcdca6f7651e1c2d1cedc2854d1b036997be85809d75bc270f8ca299.png)",
